1_statement.original:
\label{prop:chain}
If $f$ is differentiable at $a$ and $g$ is differentiable at $f(a)$, then $g\circ f$ is differentiable at $a$ and $D(g\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\,Df(a)$. If
$f$ and $g$ are $C^k$, so is $g\circ f$.

\label{lem:glnopen}
The set $\mathrm{GL}_n(\R)$ of invertible matrices is open in $M_n(\R)$, and inversion $A\mapsto A^{-1}$ is smooth on it. If $\|A-I\|\le\frac12$, then
$A$ is invertible and $\|A^{-1}\|\le2$.

\label{thm:inverse}
Let $U\subset\R^n$ be open, $k\in\{1,2,\dots,\infty\}$, $f\colon U\to\R^n$ a $C^k$ map and $a\in U$ with $Df(a)$ invertible. There are open sets $V\ni a$
and $W\ni f(a)$ such that $f$ restricts to a $C^k$ diffeomorphism $V\to W$. Its inverse $g$ satisfies $Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}$.

\label{ex:spheres}
$S^n=\{x\in\R^{n+1}:|x|=1\}$ is the zero set of $F(x)=|x|^2-1$, and $DF(x)h=2\langle x,h\rangle$ is a non-zero linear form when $x\ne0$. By (b), $S^n$ is an
$n$-dimensional submanifold of $\R^{n+1}$.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{ex:spheres}
For $n\ge0$, the sphere $S^n\subset\R^{n+1}$ is the zero set of $F(x)=|x|^2-1$. At a zero,
$DF(x)h=2\langle x,h\rangle$ is a nonzero linear form and hence surjective. Description (b) gives an $n$-dimensional submanifold, including the two isolated points of $S^0$.

\label{lem:bump}
The function $h(t)=e^{-1/t}$ for $t>0$ and $h(t)=0$ for $t\le0$ is smooth. Consequently
\[
\beta(t)=\frac{h(t)}{h(t)+h(1-t)}
\]
is smooth, equals zero for $t\le0$ and one for $t\ge1$. The function $b(x)=\beta(2-|x|)$ on $\R^n$ is smooth, lies between zero and one, equals one on $\bar B(0,1)$ and vanishes outside $B(0,2)$.

\label{thm:partition}
For every open cover $(O_i)_{i\in I}$ of a smooth manifold $M$, there is a countable family of smooth $\rho_j:M\to[0,1]$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item every support $\operatorname{supp}\rho_j=\overline{\{\rho_j\ne0\}}$ is compact and lies in some $O_i$;
\item every point has a neighbourhood meeting only finitely many supports;
\item $\sum_j\rho_j=1$.
\end{itemize}

1_statement.original:
\label{lem:hadamard}
Let $f$ be smooth on a ball $B\subset\R^n$ centred at $a$. There are smooth $g_1,\dots,g_n$ on $B$ with $g_i(a)=\partial_if(a)$ and
$f(x)=f(a)+\sum_i(x_i-a_i)g_i(x)$.

\label{thm:regularvalue}
Let $F\colon M\to N$ be smooth, $\dim M=m$, $\dim N=n$, and let $q$ be a regular value with $S=F^{-1}(q)$ non-empty. Then $S$ is an embedded submanifold of
$M$ of dimension $m-n$, and $T_pS=\ker dF_p$ for every $p\in S$.

\label{prop:embedding}
If $F\colon M\to N$ is an embedding, then $F(M)$ is an embedded submanifold of $N$, and $F$ is a diffeomorphism from $M$ onto $F(M)$. If $M$ is compact,
every injective immersion is an embedding.

\label{thm:sard}
Let $U\subset\R^n$ be open and $f\colon U\to\R^n$ a $C^1$ map, and let $C=\{x\in U:\det Df(x)=0\}$ be its set of critical points. Then $f(C)$ has
measure zero.

\label{cor:sardmfd}
Let $F\colon M\to N$ be a smooth map between manifolds of the same dimension. Its set of critical values has measure zero; in particular its regular
values are dense in $N$.

\label{prop:escape}
If $J(x_0)$ has a finite upper end $\beta$, then the maximal solution leaves every compact $K\subset U$: there is no compact $K$ with
$\varphi(t,x_0)\in K$ for all $t\in[0,\beta[$.

\label{thm:flowcont}
The flow domain $\mathcal D$ is open in $\R\times U$ and the flow $\varphi$ is continuous on it. More precisely, if $[0,T]\subset J(x_0)$, there are
$\delta>0$ and $L$ such that for $|x-x_0|<\delta$ we have $[0,T]\subset J(x)$ and $|\varphi(t,x)-\varphi(t,x_0)|\le e^{Lt}|x-x_0|$ for $t\in[0,T]$.

\label{thm:flowsmooth}
If $F$ is $C^k$, $1\le k\le\infty$, then the flow $\varphi$ is $C^k$ on $\mathcal D$. Its derivative $Y(t)=D_x\varphi(t,x)$ is the solution of the
\emph{variational equation}
\[
Y'(t)=DF(\varphi(t,x))\,Y(t),\qquad Y(0)=I.
\]

\label{ex:torusflow}
On $T^2=\R^2/\Z^2$ the constant field $X=\frac\partial{\partial\theta_1}+\alpha\frac\partial{\partial\theta_2}$ has the flow
$\varphi_t(\theta)=\theta+t(1,\alpha)$. If $\alpha=\frac pq$ is rational, every orbit closes after time $q$. If $\alpha$ is irrational, every orbit is dense:
the times $t=k\in\Z$ visit the points $(\theta_1,\theta_2+k\alpha)$, and $k\alpha$ modulo $1$ is dense (it is even equidistributed, Volume~5, Corollary~6.14). Figure~\ref{fig:torusflow}
shows an orbit for the golden slope. The orbit is the image of an injective immersion $\R\to T^2$ which is not a submanifold, a cousin of the
figure eight of Chapter~1.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{ex:torusflow}
On $T^2=\R^2/\Z^2$, the field $X=\partial_{\theta_1}+\alpha\partial_{\theta_2}$ has flow $\varphi_t(\theta)=\theta+t(1,\alpha)$ modulo integers. If $\alpha=p/q$ in lowest terms with $q>0$, the least positive period is $q$: a period must be an integer from its first coordinate, and the second then requires divisibility by $q$.

For irrational $\alpha$, integer times are dense on the vertical circle through the starting point by the irrational-rotation result of Volume~5, Corollary~6.14. To reach any other first coordinate, use times $k+s$ with one fixed $s\in[0,1)$; the second coordinates still contain the dense sequence $k\alpha$ modulo $1$. Thus the whole orbit is dense in $T^2$. Its parametrization is injective, since a repeated point would give an integer $t\ne0$ with $t\alpha$ integral, and it is an immersion because its velocity is nonzero. It is not an embedded submanifold: a submanifold is locally closed in a chart, whereas a proper dense one-dimensional subset could then be both closed and dense in a two-dimensional chart, forcing it to contain that chart. The flow gives a geometric example of the distinction from Chapter~1.

\label{ex:contact}
In $\R^3$, let $Y_1=\partial_y$ and $Y_2=\partial_x+y\partial_z$. Their span has rank two and consists of vectors $v$ satisfying $v_z-yv_x=0$. This covector is often written $\dd z-y\dd x$; Chapter~3 will develop the differential-form notation. Its bracket is $[Y_1,Y_2]=\partial_z$, which is outside the plane at every point. No local surface can be tangent to these planes: fields tangent to a submanifold have bracket tangent there, since they and their commutator kill its local defining functions. Here that would require the missing $\partial_z$ direction to be tangent as well.

The small-loop formula shows how a loop of allowed motions gains a displacement in this missing direction. In this three-dimensional example the two fields and their bracket span every tangent direction. Nonintegrability alone in an arbitrary larger space would not imply that full spanning property. Exercise~\ref{ex:parking} computes an idealized vehicle model in which the distinction can be seen directly.

1_statement.original:
\label{prop:volumeform}
A manifold of dimension $n\ge1$ is orientable if and only if it carries a nowhere-vanishing $n$-form. Such a form $\mu$ determines the orientation of the
charts in which $\mu=a\,\dd x^1\wedge\dots\wedge\dd x^n$ with $a>0$.

\label{prop:intwelldef}
The integral is well defined and linear. If $F\colon N\to M$ is a diffeomorphism that preserves orientation, $\int_NF^*\omega=\int_M\omega$; if it reverses it, the sign
changes.

\label{prop:bdryorient}
Let $M$ be oriented and $n\ge2$. The charts $(x^2,\dots,x^n)$ of $\partial M$ obtained by restricting oriented charts of $M$ are orientation-compatible, and define the
\emph{induced orientation} on $\partial M$: ``outward normal first''.

\label{thm:h1punctured}
A closed $1$-form $\omega$ on $\R^2\setminus\{0\}$ is exact if and only if $\int_{S^1}\omega=0$. Hence $H^1(\R^2\setminus\{0\})$ is one-dimensional, spanned by the class of
$\dd\theta$, and the isomorphism with $\R$ is $\omega\mapsto\frac1{2\pi}\int_{S^1}\omega$.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{thm:h1punctured}
A closed $1$-form $\omega$ on the punctured plane is exact precisely when $\int_{S^1}\omega=0$. Its first de Rham cohomology is one-dimensional, spanned by $[\dd\theta]$, and its isomorphism with $\R$ is
$[\omega]\mapsto(2\pi)^{-1}\int_{S^1}\omega$.

1_statement.original:
\label{cor:windingintegral}
For a closed piecewise $C^1$ curve $\gamma$ in $\R^2\setminus\{a\}$, the winding number of Volume~7 (Definition~6.5, after Lemma~6.4) is
$n(\gamma,a)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_\gamma\dd\theta_a$, where $\dd\theta_a$ is $\dd\theta$ translated to $a$.

\label{ex:curvexamples}
For the sphere, $\dd\phi^2+\sin^2\phi\,\dd\vartheta^2$ gives $a=1$, $b=\sin\phi$, and $K=-\frac1{\sin\phi}(\cos\phi)_\phi=1$. For the hyperbolic half-plane, $a=b=\frac1y$ gives
$\frac{a_y}b=-\frac1y$, whose $y$-derivative is $\frac1{y^2}$, and $K=-y^2\cdot\frac1{y^2}=-1$. For the plane in polar coordinates, $\dd r^2+r^2\dd\vartheta^2$, $K=-\frac1r(1)_r=0$, as it should be.

\label{thm:egregium}
For an oriented surface $S\subset\R^3$, $K=\det(dN)$. In particular $\det(dN)$, which is defined by how the surface sits in space, depends only on the metric of
$S$, and is preserved by isometries.

\label{lem:index}
The index does not depend on the radius of the circle, nor on the oriented chart. It can also be computed in any oriented orthonormal frame $(f_1,f_2)$
defined on a disc around $p$: if $X=|X|(\cos\psi f_1+\sin\psi f_2)$, then $\operatorname{ind}_p(X)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_c\dd\psi$.

\label{lem:morseindex}
Let $f$ be a smooth function on a Riemannian surface and $p$ a non-degenerate critical point. Then $p$ is an isolated zero of $\operatorname{grad}f$ (the vector field with
$\langle\operatorname{grad}f,v\rangle=\dd f(v)$), and its index is the sign of $\det$ of the Hessian: $+1$ at a local maximum or minimum, $-1$ at a saddle.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{cor:chimorse}
A Morse function on a compact oriented surface without boundary satisfies
$\chi(M)=\#\{\text{minima}\}-\#\{\text{saddles}\}+\#\{\text{maxima}\}$.

\label{ex:chiexamples}
The sphere's height function has a nondegenerate minimum and maximum, so $\chi(S^2)=2$ and its unit-radius curvature integral is $4\pi$. The torus has a nowhere-zero coordinate field $\partial_{\theta_1}$, giving $\chi(T^2)=0$. Therefore any metric on it has either identically zero curvature or curvature of both signs: a nonzero continuous function of only one sign could not integrate to zero.

We quote the standard Morse-function construction on the connected genus-$g$ handle surface: it admits one minimum, one maximum and $2g$ saddles. The index formula then gives $2-2g$. The construction and the smooth classification of closed oriented surfaces by genus are quoted here; Milnor's \emph{Morse Theory} develops the relevant handle constructions. Chapter~6 will recover the same Euler characteristic from homology and the alternating count $V-E+F$ of a triangulation. Those later descriptions are comparisons, not premises for the curvature proof.

1_statement.original:
\label{prop:green}
On a compact oriented surface without boundary, $\int_Mg\,\Delta f\dd A=-\int_M\langle\dd g,\dd f\rangle\dd A$. Hence $-\Delta$ is symmetric and non-negative, its eigenvalues
($\Delta f=-\lambda f$, $f\ne0$) satisfy $\lambda\ge0$, and if $M$ is connected the eigenvalue $0$ has only the constants as eigenfunctions.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{prop:green}
On a compact oriented surface without boundary,
$\int_M g\Delta f\,dA=-\int_M\langle dg,df\rangle dA$ for smooth real $f,g$. Consequently $-\Delta$ is symmetric and nonnegative. Every eigenvalue $\lambda$ in $\Delta f=-\lambda f$, $f\ne0$, is real and nonnegative, and on a connected surface its zero eigenfunctions are exactly the constants.

\label{thm:torusspectrum}
On $T^2=\R^2/\Z^2$ with metric $dx^2+dy^2$, the eigenvalues of $-\Delta$ are $4\pi^2n$ for integers $n=k_1^2+k_2^2$, $k_1,k_2\in\Z$. Their multiplicities are
$r_2(n)=\#\{(k_1,k_2)\in\Z^2:k_1^2+k_2^2=n\}$. The functions $e_k(x)=e^{2\pi i\langle k,x\rangle}$ form an orthonormal eigenbasis of $L^2(T^2)$.

1_statement.original:
\label{lem:convexhomotopy}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Two paths with the same end points in a convex subset of $\R^n$ are homotopic, by the straight-line homotopy $H(t,s)=(1-s)\gamma_0(t)+s\gamma_1(t)$.
\item[(b)] If $f\colon X\to Y$ is a map and $\gamma_0\simeq\gamma_1$ in $X$, then $f\circ\gamma_0\simeq f\circ\gamma_1$ in $Y$.
\item[(c)] (Reparametrization.) If $\varphi\colon I\to I$ is continuous with $\varphi(0)=0$ and $\varphi(1)=1$, then $\gamma\circ\varphi\simeq\gamma$.
\end{enumerate}

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{lem:convexhomotopy}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Paths with the same endpoints in a convex subset of $\R^n$ are homotopic through $H(t,s)=(1-s)\gamma_0(t)+s\gamma_1(t)$.
\item[(b)] If $\gamma_0\simeq\gamma_1$ in $X$ and $f:X\to Y$, then $f\circ\gamma_0\simeq f\circ\gamma_1$ in $Y$.
\item[(c)] If $\varphi:I\to I$ is continuous, $\varphi(0)=0$ and $\varphi(1)=1$, then $\gamma\circ\varphi\simeq\gamma$.
\end{enumerate}

1_statement.original:
\label{prop:pi1group}
Whenever the products are defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] if $\gamma\simeq\gamma'$ and $\delta\simeq\delta'$ then $\gamma\cdot\delta\simeq\gamma'\cdot\delta'$;
\item[(b)] $(\gamma\cdot\delta)\cdot\varepsilon\simeq\gamma\cdot(\delta\cdot\varepsilon)$;
\item[(c)] $c_{\gamma(0)}\cdot\gamma\simeq\gamma\simeq\gamma\cdot c_{\gamma(1)}$;
\item[(d)] $\gamma\cdot\bar\gamma\simeq c_{\gamma(0)}$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the homotopy classes of loops at $x_0$ form a group under $[\gamma][\delta]=[\gamma\cdot\delta]$, with identity $[c_{x_0}]$ and inverse $[\gamma]^{-1}=[\bar\gamma]$: the
\emph{fundamental group} $\pi_1(X,x_0)$.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{prop:pi1group}
For paths whose indicated products exist:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\gamma\simeq\gamma'$ and $\delta\simeq\delta'$ imply $\gamma\cdot\delta\simeq\gamma'\cdot\delta'$;
\item[(b)] $(\gamma\cdot\delta)\cdot\varepsilon\simeq\gamma\cdot(\delta\cdot\varepsilon)$;
\item[(c)] $c_{\gamma(0)}\cdot\gamma\simeq\gamma\simeq\gamma\cdot c_{\gamma(1)}$;
\item[(d)] $\gamma\cdot\bar\gamma\simeq c_{\gamma(0)}$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently loop classes at $x_0$ form the \emph{fundamental group} $\pi_1(X,x_0)$, with product $[\gamma][\delta]=[\gamma\cdot\delta]$, identity $[c_{x_0}]$ and inverse $[\bar\gamma]$.

1_statement.original:
\label{prop:homotopyinv}
Let $F\colon X\times I\to Y$ be a homotopy from $f=F(\cdot,0)$ to $g=F(\cdot,1)$, and let $h(s)=F(x_0,s)$. Then $g_*=\beta_h\circ f_*$. Consequently, if $f\colon X\to Y$ is a
\emph{homotopy equivalence}, that is, if there is $g\colon Y\to X$ with $g\circ f$ and $f\circ g$ homotopic to the identities, then $f_*$ is an isomorphism.

\label{thm:homotopylifting}
Let $p\colon\tilde X\to X$ be a covering, $F\colon I\times I\to X$ a map, and $\tilde x$ a point with $p(\tilde x)=F(0,0)$. There is a unique lift $\tilde F$ of $F$ with $\tilde F(0,0)=\tilde x$. In particular
(taking $F(t,s)=\gamma(t)$) every path $\gamma$ has a unique lift $\tilde\gamma$ starting at any given point over $\gamma(0)$.

\label{cor:liftendpoints}
If $\gamma_0\simeq\gamma_1$ and $\tilde\gamma_0,\tilde\gamma_1$ are their lifts from the same point, then $\tilde\gamma_0(1)=\tilde\gamma_1(1)$ and $\tilde\gamma_0\simeq\tilde\gamma_1$. Consequently
$p_*\colon\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)\to\pi_1(X,p(\tilde x_0))$ is injective.

\label{thm:pi1circle}
$\pi_1(S^1,1)\cong\Z$. The isomorphism sends the class of a loop $\gamma$ to $\tilde\gamma(1)$, where $\tilde\gamma$ is the lift of $\gamma$ to $\R$ starting at $0$ for the covering $p(t)=e^{2\pi it}$, and the
class of $\omega_n(t)=e^{2\pi int}$ to $n$.

\label{prop:fibre}
Let $p\colon\tilde X\to X$ be a covering, $\tilde X$ path-connected, $p(\tilde x_0)=x_0$ and $H=p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)$. The action of $\pi_1(X,x_0)$ on the fibre is transitive, and the stabilizer
of $\tilde x_0$ is $H$. So $\tilde x_0\cdot g\mapsto Hg$ is a bijection from the fibre to the set of right cosets of $H$, and the number of sheets is the index $[\pi_1(X,x_0):H]$.

\label{thm:liftcrit}
Let $p\colon(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)\to(X,x_0)$ be a covering and $f\colon(Y,y_0)\to(X,x_0)$ a map, $Y$ path-connected and locally path-connected. A lift $\tilde f$ with $\tilde f(y_0)=\tilde x_0$ exists if and only
if $f_*\pi_1(Y,y_0)\subset p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)$, and it is then unique.

\label{thm:deck}
Let $p\colon\tilde X\to X$ be a covering with $\tilde X$ path-connected, $G=\pi_1(X,x_0)$, and $H=p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $\tilde x_1=\tilde x_0\cdot g$, then $p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_1)=g^{-1}Hg$.
\item[(b)] There is a deck transformation $\tau$ with $\tau(\tilde x_0)=\tilde x_0\cdot g$ if and only if $g$ lies in the normalizer $N(H)$ of $H$.
\item[(c)] $\operatorname{Deck}(p)\cong N(H)/H$. In particular $p$ is normal if and only if $H$ is a normal subgroup, and then $\operatorname{Deck}(p)\cong G/H$; for a universal covering,
$\operatorname{Deck}(p)\cong G$.
\end{enumerate}

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{thm:deck}
For a covering with path-connected $\tilde X$, write $G=\pi_1(X,x_0)$ and $H=p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_0)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The relation $\tilde x_1=\tilde x_0\cdot g$ gives $p_*\pi_1(\tilde X,\tilde x_1)=g^{-1}Hg$.
\item[(b)] A deck transformation taking $\tilde x_0$ to $\tilde x_0\cdot g$ exists exactly when $g\in N(H)$, the normalizer of $H$.
\item[(c)] $\operatorname{Deck}(p)\cong N(H)/H$. The covering is normal exactly when $H$ is normal in $G$; then its deck group is $G/H$. A universal covering has deck group $G$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{thm:galois}
Suppose $X$ is path-connected, locally path-connected and semilocally simply connected. For every $H\le\pi_1(X,x_0)$ there is a covering
$p_H:(X_H,\tilde x_0)\to(X,x_0)$ with path-connected $X_H$ and $p_{H*}\pi_1(X_H,\tilde x_0)=H$. Two based coverings with these properties and the same subgroup have a unique basepoint-preserving isomorphism. The subgroup $\{1\}$ gives a universal covering.

1_statement.original:
\label{prop:attachdisc}
If $X$ is path-connected, $\pi_1(X\cup_\varphi D^2)\cong\pi_1(X)/\langle\langle[\varphi]\rangle\rangle$, the quotient by the normal subgroup generated by the class of the loop $\varphi$ (moved to the base point
by any path).

\label{lem:trees}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $v$ is a vertex of a graph $Y$ lying on a single edge end, of an edge $e$ that is not a loop, then $Y$ deformation retracts onto $Y\setminus(\{v\}\cup e^\circ)$, where $e^\circ$ is the open edge.
\item[(b)] A finite tree with $V$ vertices has $V-1$ edges and deformation retracts onto any of its vertices; a finite tree with one extra edge is homotopy equivalent to a circle.
\item[(c)] Every finite connected graph has a spanning tree.
\end{enumerate}

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{lem:trees}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If a vertex $v$ has just one incident edge end, on a nonloop edge $e$, then $Y$ deformation retracts onto $Y\setminus(\{v\}\cup e^\circ)$, where $e^\circ$ is the edge interior.
\item[(b)] A finite tree with $V$ vertices has $V-1$ edges and deformation retracts onto any chosen vertex. A finite tree with one extra edge is homotopy equivalent to a circle.
\item[(c)] A spanning tree exists in every finite connected graph.
\end{enumerate}

1_statement.original:
\label{thm:nielsenschreier}
Every subgroup $H$ of the free group $F_n$ is free. If $H$ has finite index $k$, its rank is
\[1+k(n-1).\]

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{lem:five}
In a commutative diagram of abelian groups with exact rows,
\[
\begin{array}{ccccccccc}
A_1&\to&A_2&\to&A_3&\to&A_4&\to&A_5\\
\downarrow\alpha_1&&\downarrow\alpha_2&&\downarrow\alpha_3&&\downarrow\alpha_4&&\downarrow\alpha_5\\
B_1&\to&B_2&\to&B_3&\to&B_4&\to&B_5,
\end{array}
\]
if $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_4,\alpha_5$ are isomorphisms, then $\alpha_3$ is an isomorphism too.

1_statement.original:
\label{thm:spheres}
$\tilde H_k(S^n;R)\cong R$ for $k=n$ and $0$ otherwise. More precisely, let $U_\pm$ be the open sets $\pm x_{n+1}>-\frac12$ of $S^n$ and $S^{n-1}$ the equator $x_{n+1}=0$. The Mayer--Vietoris map
$\delta\colon\tilde H_k(S^n)\to\tilde H_{k-1}(U_+\cap U_-)\cong\tilde H_{k-1}(S^{n-1})$ is an isomorphism, natural for maps $S^n\to S^n$ that preserve $U_+$, $U_-$ and the equator.

\label{cor:brouwerH}
There is no retraction of the closed ball $\bar B^{n+1}$ onto $S^n$, and every map of $\bar B^{n+1}$ to itself has a fixed point. If $m\ne n$ ($m,n\ge1$), no nonempty open subset of $\R^m$ is homeomorphic to an open
subset of $\R^n$.

\label{cor:euler}
The Betti numbers and the Euler characteristic of a finite simplicial complex depend only on the homotopy type of $|K|$. In particular all triangulations of a space have the same $V-E+F-\cdots$,
and for every convex polyhedron in $\R^3$, $V-E+F=2$.

1_statement.reviewed:
\label{prop:jordanbrouwer}
Let $n\ge1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $h:\bar B^k\to S^n$ is an embedding, then $\tilde H_i(S^n\setminus h(\bar B^k))=0$ in every degree.
\item[(b)] If $h:S^k\to S^n$ is an embedding with $0\le k<n$, then $\tilde H_i(S^n\setminus h(S^k))$ is $\Z$ for $i=n-k-1$ and zero otherwise.
\end{enumerate}
In particular an embedded $S^{n-1}$ has a complement with exactly two path components, whereas an embedded closed ball has path-connected complement. An embedding means a homeomorphism onto its image; for these compact domains, a continuous injection into $S^n$ is an embedding.

